{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795819426,"nonce":"RnUm5HAcA/sq+kN3PvoMr6k5TGw=","title":"A node with an id returns to its first props and layout at the next state pass after an update","message":"The state pass keeps the props and layout style a node returns to when its\nstate rules stop applying (`base_styles`, `base_taffy_styles`). It reads them\nonce. An update that replaces an element in place writes new props and a new\nlayout style but keeps the old saved copies, unless the node's classes also\nchanged. The next pass puts the old copies back.\n\nIt affects any node with an id, which includes the children of a stateful with\ngenerated ids, whenever the stylesheet has a state rule. After an update from\n`.w(100).bg(red)` to `.w(200).bg(blue)`, a hover, press or focus change\nanywhere leaves the node 100 wide and red. In a headless session, a child with\nan id inside a stateful keeps its first color while the stateful is hovered.\n\nSignal-bound writes are not affected: they also write to the saved copies.","files":null}]}